  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Gaigel (German trick-taking). Setup: 4 players in two partnerships. 48-card German deck (WÃ¼rttemberg pattern). 12 cards dealt (3 then 2 batches). 21st card shown as trump. Gameplay: Two phases: (1) Talon phase: players can play any card, no suit-following. (2) Talon empty: must follow suit, must trump if unable. Marriages (K+O of same suit) declare for 20 (non-trump) or 40 (trump) points. Scoring: Ace=11, 10=10, K=4, O=3, U=2, 7=0. Win: First team to 101 points.',
    Player: "Welcome to Gaigel, where German tradition meets strategy. You and your partner must work together to reach 101 points first. Early in the hand, you can play any cardâ€”use this freedom wisely. When the Talon runs dry, you must follow suit or trump. Declare marriages (matching King and Ober) for 20 or 40 points. Count carefully, and call 'Out!' when you believe you've reached 101.",
    objective: 'The aim of the play is to take tricks containing valuable cards and to declare King-Ober marriages; the first team that reaches 101 or more points wins the round and must stop the play by calling Aus to claim the win.',
    gameplay: 'Gaigel proceeds through deal, legal play actions, and end-of-round resolution. Play proceeds through legal actions until round resolution.',
    keyRules: [
      'Out! (Schluss!): A player can stop the game midndhand if they believe their team has reached 101 points. If they are correct, they win. If they are wrong, the opponents win regardless of the final score.',
      'Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Points scored through tricks and marriage declarations.',
      'Winning condition: First team to reach 101 points wins the round.',
    ],
